2. Minimal C++ implementation

Suppose MATLAB gives you three tones for the regime you want:

f1, A1, phi1
f2, A2, phi2
f3, A3, phi3

Then the C++ implementation can be essentially this:

const double pi = 3.14159265358979323846;
const int number_of_tones = 3;
const double tone_frequency[number_of_tones] = {
    FREQ_1,
    FREQ_2,
    FREQ_3
};
const double tone_amplitude[number_of_tones] = {
    AMP_1,
    AMP_2,
    AMP_3
};
const double tone_phase[number_of_tones] = {
    PHASE_1,
    PHASE_2,
    PHASE_3
};
const double bias = BIAS_VALUE;
/* Generate sensor error */
double sensor_error = bias;
for (int j = 0; j < number_of_tones; ++j)
{
    sensor_error +=
        tone_amplitude[j] *
        sin(2.0*pi*tone_frequency[j]*global_time +
            tone_phase[j]);
}

Then inject sensor_error in the same location where your existing sensor model error is applied.

There is no filter state anymore. Every time step simply evaluates

A_j\sin(2\pi f_jt+\phi_j)

using the current simulation time.

What disappears from your current C++ implementation

You can therefore remove the entire stochastic-filter path:

randn()
white_excitation
noise_model_A
noise_model_B
noise_output_history
noise_input_history
AR/ARMA recurrence
burn-in
CSV coefficient loading

You retain only:

simulation time
       |
       v
A1*sin(2*pi*f1*t + phi1)
A2*sin(2*pi*f2*t + phi2)
...
       |
       v
sum tones + bias
       |
       v
inject sensor error

One thing I would not remove automatically is your existing frame transformation/injection logic. The tone model should replace the generation of the sensor error, while whatever downstream code puts that scalar/vector error into the appropriate sensor/platform frame can remain unchanged.

Also, your earlier full model was intentionally

\text{bias}+\text{tones}+\text{colored stochastic residual}.

This simplified version is

\text{bias}+\text{tones}.

So this is a useful baseline/trade study, but you’re deliberately throwing away all of the measured broadband stochastic residual. If the objective is specifically “How much of the behavior can I reproduce using just the dominant deterministic spectral components?”, this is exactly the simple architecture I would test first.
